{"id":"same-proposal-by-district","title":"Same kind of proposal, different district","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Does the same kind of application fare differently depending on the supervisor district it is in?","finding":"After giving every district the same mix of proposals, the share not approved runs from 23.3% in District 7 (214 decided) to 33.6% in District 11 (157) against 26.9% citywide; 1 of 11 districts sits further from what its own mix predicts than chance would explain (District 11: 70 not approved against 52 expected).","how":"Each application is placed by its parcel (Assessor’s roll: 5,350 of 5,392 placed). “Same mix” is direct standardization: the district’s own rate for each combination of approval type (conditional use, variance, other) and kind of proposal (eight groups) is weighted by how common that combination is citywide; a combination with fewer than 5 local cases takes the city rate, which pulls small districts toward the middle. “Count its own mix predicts” applies the citywide rate for the same approval type, kind and period (2015-19, 2020-26) to each of the district’s own cases; “beyond chance” compares the two counts at about a 1-in-20 level shared across the 11 districts. Review requests are public discretionary-review filings per 1,000 full (not over-the-counter) building permits on homes and apartment buildings. “Reviews decided” counts discretionary-review hearings with a recorded result, whether a neighbor asked or the code required one; “sent back with changes” means the Planning Commission took review and approved the project with modifications. “Appeals upheld” is the wording of the record. Cells under 20 cases are blank.","limits":"A difference between places is not proof of bias. Places differ in what is proposed, in zoning, lot sizes, building ages and land prices, and in who chooses to apply at all; applicants also withdraw for their own reasons (money, a sale, a redesign filed under a new number). Standardizing for kind of proposal does not equalize zoning or lot conditions inside a kind: a rear-yard variance on a steep narrow lot is not the same request as one on a flat wide lot. Districts are drawn by current lines. The hearing minutes, roll-call votes and appeal decisions now being collected would show who voted, who objected and what reason was recorded in each case.","columns":[{"k":"area","t":"Place","type":"text"},{"k":"decided","t":"Applications decided","type":"int"},{"k":"notApproved","t":"Not approved (count)","type":"int"},{"k":"expected","t":"Count its own mix predicts","type":"int"},{"k":"raw","t":"Not approved","type":"pct"},{"k":"adjusted","t":"Not approved, same mix of proposals","type":"pct"},{"k":"gap","t":"Gap from the city rate","type":"pct"},{"k":"beyondChance","t":"Beyond chance?","type":"text"},{"k":"denied","t":"Refused outright","type":"pct"},{"k":"reviewPer1000","t":"Neighbors’ review requests per 1,000 full residential permits","type":"int"},{"k":"heard","t":"Reviews decided","type":"int"},{"k":"changed","t":"Sent back with changes","type":"pct"},{"k":"appeals","t":"Appeals decided","type":"int"},{"k":"appealUpheld","t":"Appeals upheld","type":"pct"},{"k":"permitAdjusted","t":"Full building permits withdrawn, cancelled or refused (adjusted)","type":"pct"},{"k":"homesLost","t":"Proposed homes in projects denied or withdrawn","type":"pct"}],"rows":[{"area":"District 11","key":"11","decided":157,"notApproved":70,"raw":0.4459,"adjusted":0.3358,"expected":51.6,"ratio":1.36,"adjFirm":0.3318,"firmShare":0.707,"gap":0.0673,"beyondChance":"yes","z":3.16,"denied":0.0191,"reviewPer1000":20.7,"reviewRequests":26,"fullPermits":1256,"heard":14,"changed":null,"deniedAtReview":null,"appeals":9,"appealUpheld":null,"permitsDecided":1278,"permitNotIssued":0.1847,"permitAdjusted":0.1789,"homesProposed":1861,"homesDecided":839,"homesApproved":621,"homesLost":0.2598,"homesBuilt":1201,"affordableBuilt":412,"income":130684,"renters":0.3399,"white":0.1376,"asian":0.5357,"black":0.